<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#iron-curtain">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iron Curtain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iron Curtain was the political, military and physical boundary that divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, separating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the communist states of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> from the West. The phrase was made famous by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> in a speech at Fulton, Missouri, in March 1946. In many places the division became a fortified border of fences, minefields and watchtowers, of which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a> was the best-known part, and it fell with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1989">revolutions of 1989</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The phrase was used before the Cold War: Queen Elisabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> used it in 1914 for a barrier descending between her people and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Perhaps the first recorded application to Soviet Russia is in Vasily Rozanov's 1918 polemic The Apocalypse of Our Time.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a>'s first recorded use was in a telegram of 12 May 1945 to President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman">Harry S. Truman</a>, expressing concern about Soviet actions, and he repeated it on 4 June and in the House of Commons on 16 August 1945. On 5 March 1946, at Westminster College in Fulton, Missouri, with Truman attending, he said that from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> in the Baltic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> in the Adriatic an iron curtain had descended across the continent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The antagonism behind the term had several origins. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a> of August 1939 contained a secret agreement to split <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and Nazi Germany, and the Soviets then occupied eastern Poland, the Baltic states and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. Between 1945 and 1949 the Soviets converted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, Poland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, Romania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> into satellite states, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> turned away from the Soviet Union in the late 1940s.</p><p>The curtain took physical shape as border defences, among the most heavily militarised areas in the world, particularly the inner German border. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> a double barbed-wire fence was set 50 metres from the border with mines between the fences, later replaced by an electric signal fence, guard towers, a sand strip to track violations and patrols authorised to use weapons to stop escapees. Emigration from east to west was effectively halted after 1950, after more than 15 million people, mainly ethnic Germans, had left Soviet-occupied countries in the five years following the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Churchill's speech was not well received at the time, and much of the Western public still regarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> as a close ally. The phrase gained popularity as a shorthand for the division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> progressed, and the curtain served to keep people in and information out.</p><p>Its end began with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. East Germany reunited with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> on 3 October 1990, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> dissolved in December 1991 and Czechoslovakia in 1992. The fall of the curtain was accompanied by a large rise in east–west migration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iron Curtain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
